erson': [{'firstname': 'Gretchen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Morgenson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d1370377-52ea-577c-8dcb-8eb26732a977', 'word_count': 1199, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d1370377-52ea-577c-8dcb-8eb26732a977'}, {'abstract': 'Kelly Behun’s new capsule collection for Barneys will be unveiled at a gallery-like pop-up shop beginning Sept. 8.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/01/t-magazine/design/kelly-behun-barneys-pop-up.html', 'snippet': 'Kelly Behun’s new capsule collection for Barneys will be unveiled at a gallery-like pop-up shop beginning Sept. 8.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In an age when stores look increasingly like  homes — cozy and well-appointed spaces to hang, relax, stay awhile — it comes as little surprise that the interior designer Kelly Behun’s newest pop-up shop at Barneys New York would be outfitted with one-of-a-kind furniture, custom wallpaper and art. The focal point of the shop was once installed in the late gallerist Holly Solomon’s own dining room: a bespoke, floor-to-ceiling neoprene panel, painted for Solomon by the artist Izhar Patkin. “It was this very traditional theme of pastoral picnic and yet it was on this crazy very subversive material, and the fact that she would have that in her Park Avenue apartment — I was so intrigued by,” Behun says, adding that the space itself is a homage to Solomon, who blurred the lines between art, craft and design in the ’70s and ’80s. “It comes back to this idea of living with art and how you incorporate art and design in your world. And you create these sort of tableaus in your home that hopefully enrich you in some way.”', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/01/t-magazine/01tmag-behun/01tmag-behu