d former spy, over the years.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Years ago, when he was thinking about writing an autobiography, John le Carré recounts, he hired two detectives to research him and his family. As the son of a flamboyant con man, as a spy for Her Majesty’s Secret Service, and as a novelist who spent his days making up things, truth and memory tended to blur together: “I’m a liar, I explained. Born to lying, bred to it, trained to it by an industry that lies for a living, practiced in it as a novelist.” He was interested in learning the facts of his life, he told the detectives — since, “as a maker of fictions, I invent versions of myself, never the real thing, if it exists.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/02/arts/02BOOK1/02BOOK1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/09/02/arts/02BOOK1/02BOOK1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/02/arts/02BOOK1/02BOOK1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/02/arts/02BOOK1/02BOOK1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 435, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/09/02/arts/02BOOK1/02BOOK1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 435}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/02/arts/02BOOK1/02BOOK1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 743, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/02/arts/02BOOK1/02BOOK1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1486, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: Le Carré, the Con Man’s Son: Writer, Liar, Survivor, Spy', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Writer, Liar, Survivor, Spy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Le Carre, John', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Pigeon Tunnel: Stories From My Life (Book)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-09-01T18:37:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michiko Kakutani', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michiko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kakutani', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4038ac92-9691-5d81-a593-b0b1e10fcbbf', 'word_count': 1259, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4038ac92-9691-5d81-a593-b0b1e10fcbbf'}, {'abstract': 'New York is changing a rule that requires an entire family to be present at the city’s intake center when reapplying to get a spot in a shelter.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/02/nyregion/new-york-homeless-families-path-policy.html', 'snippet': 'New York is changing a rule that requires an entire family to be present at the city’s intake center when reapplying to get a spot in a shelter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Homeless families in New York City have long been forced into a difficult choice: In order to apply for housing, they must have their children skip school.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/02/nyregion/02PATH/02PATH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/09/02/nyregion/02PATH/02PATH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/02/nyregion/02PATH/02PATH-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/02/nyregion/02PATH/02PATH-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/09/02/nyregion/02PATH/02PATH-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/02/nyregion/02PATH/02PATH-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/02/nyregion/02PATH/02PATH-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Under New Policy for Homeless Families, Children Can Miss Less School', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'City Alters a School Policy to Aid Homeless Families', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homeless Persons', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Families and Family Life', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-09-01T18:50:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elizabeth A. Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elizabeth', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4435e5f5-9c53-516e-90ae-c43fe5a41b48', 'word_count': 795, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4435e5f5-9c53-516e-90ae-c43fe5a41b48'}, {'abstract': 'Drug makers have spent $2.3 billion lobbying Congress over the last decade, which may explain why lawmakers have done little to curb drug prices.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/02/business/dealbook/rising-drug-prices-put-big-pharmas-lobbying-to-the-test.html', 'snippet': 'Drug makers have spent $2.3 billion lobbying Congress over the last decade, which may explain why lawmakers have done little to curb drug prices.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Drug makers have been getting their $2.3 billion worth in Washington. That is how much they have spent lobbying Congress over the last decade. It may help explain why no legislative proposal to rein in rising prescription prices has gone anywhere. The latest outcry involving Mylan will put that hefty investment in influence to its biggest test.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rising Drug Prices Put Big Pharma’s Lobbying to the Test', 'kicker': 'Breakingviews', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drugs (Pharmaceuticals)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prices (Fares, Fees and Rates)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lobbying and Lobbyists', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Mylan Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-09-01T18:51:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'DealBook', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gina Chon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0144b626-be00-5200-9de1-5d00545cf5b6', 'word_count': 368, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0144b626-be00-5200-9de1-5d00545cf5b6'}, {'abstract': 'UrbanGlass in Fort Greene, Brooklyn, gives glass blowers access to the space and expensive equipment they need to practice their craft.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/04/nyregion/urbanglass-in-fort-greene-brooklyn-glass-blowers-share-studio-space.html', 'snippet': 'UrbanGlass in Fort Greene, Brooklyn, gives glass blowers access to the space and expensive equipment they need to practice their craft.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Even when temperatures reach 90 degrees, a certain type of New Yorker will choose to stand next to a 2,000-degree furnace. This is a glass blower. Since few of them are able to set up shop in their apartments, many head to UrbanGlass, which started in 1977 as an artists’ co-op in SoHo called the New York Experimental Glass Workshop. Under its current name and at its current location in Fort Greene, UrbanGlass gives glass artists, and those who try to be, access to the expensive and enormous equipment they need.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For the Sake of Art: Risk and Reward at 2,000 Degrees', 'kicker': 'Neighborhood Joint', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'If You Can Stand the Heat', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Glass', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Textiles', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'SoHo (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'UrbanGlass', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Fort Greene (Brooklyn, NY)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-09-01T19:06:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metropolitan', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gloria Dawson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gloria', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dawson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7c95e689-67b0-5acd-ab60-b5fd368c1e40', 'word_count': 614, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7c95e689-67b0-5acd-ab60-b5fd368c1e40'}, {'abstract': 'The campaign is a major shift for a country whose government wrestled with how to regulate the energy industry.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/02/business/energy-environment/israel-courts-foreign-money-in-effort-to-become-gas-exporter.html', 'snippet': 'The campaign is a major shift for a country whose government wrestled with how to regulate the energy industry.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Israel’s energy minister laid on the charm as he spoke to a few dozen oil executives, contractors and analysts in a crowded hotel conference room about the country’s natural gas prospects.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/01/business/02israelgas/02israelgas-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/09/01/business/02israelgas/02israelgas-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'capt