nt_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles Isherwood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Isherwood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/611031b9-8a5b-5ad4-9700-6ee61c43192f', 'word_count': 936, 'uri': 'nyt://article/611031b9-8a5b-5ad4-9700-6ee61c43192f'}, {'abstract': 'Carl Hiaasen’s latest novel weaves together a demoted detective, a reality-TV star and Florida skulduggery in the writer’s trademark comic style.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/05/books/razor-girl-a-tale-of-kidnappers-reality-tv-stars-and-dodgy-seafood.html', 'snippet': 'Carl Hiaasen’s latest novel weaves together a demoted detective, a reality-TV star and Florida skulduggery in the writer’s trademark comic style.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Carl Hiaasen’s irresistible “Razor Girl” meets his usual sky-high standards for elegance, craziness and mike-drop humor. But this election-year novel is exceptionally timely, too. It illustrates the dog-whistle effects of bigotry that take the form of entertainment, with a plot that revolves around a “Duck Dynasty”-type reality show, the sermons delivered by one of its stars and a crazed fan who decides to follow what he thinks are the star’s teachings. Mr. Hiaasen — and probably only Mr. Hiaasen — could weave this into a book that’s still so funny.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/05/arts/05BOOKHIAASEN2/05BOOKHIAASEN2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/09/05/arts/05BOOKHIAASEN2/05BOOKHIAASEN2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0,