minant.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There can be a pronounced disconnect between what happens on a practice court in preparation to win a tennis match, and what actually happens once the match begins.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/07/sports/07-Y-SCORE/07-Y-SCORE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/09/07/sports/07-Y-SCORE/07-Y-SCORE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/07/sports/07-Y-SCORE/07-Y-SCORE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/07/sports/07-Y-SCORE/07-Y-SCORE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 389, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/09/07/sports/07-Y-SCORE/07-Y-SCORE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 389}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/07/sports/07-Y-SCORE/07-Y-SCORE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 663, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/07/sports/07-Y-SCORE/07-Y-SCORE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1327, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Key to Winning at the U.S. Open Is in the First Four Shots', 'kicker': 'Keeping Score', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tennis Coaches Take Note: Open Winners Score Early', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Open (Tennis)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-09-06T09:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': "By Craig O'Shannessy", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Craig', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "O'Shannessy", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aa4c13fe-f321-5038-a4e2-8baf57b4155d', 'word_count': 467, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aa4c13fe-f321-5038-a4e2-8baf57b4155d'}, {'abstract': 'And what exactly are we looking for?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/11/magazine/how-much-disclosure-do-we-really-expect-from-candidates.html', 'snippet': 'And what exactly are we looking for?', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s a safe bet that advisers to Hillary Clinton spend more time than they would like batting away the suggestion that their candidate must be hiding something. This is what Joel Benenson, Clinton’s chief campaign strategist, had to do on CNN one morning in late August, when the anchor Chris Cuomo confronted him with questions about Clinton’s meetings with donors to the Clinton Foundation, in the years when she was secretary of state. The subtext was whether Clinton had been forthcoming enough about the influence of her private connections on her exercise of official power. Benenson, like any good surrogate, tried to change the subject. “Let’s talk about Donald Trump,” he said. “The man who said, ‘I will release my tax returns if I run for president.’ Who knows what we’ll find if he releases those tax returns? I think if you want to be hammering somebody in this race day in and day out about disclosure,” he added, it should be Trump.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail',