h': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Summer Reading Contest Winner, Week 10 | On ‘The Age of Post-Truth Politics’', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2016-09-06T10:30:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3100384f-522d-5bcd-9d94-7cd3f0e4b1a1', 'word_count': 1012, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3100384f-522d-5bcd-9d94-7cd3f0e4b1a1'}, {'abstract': 'The Philippines’ new president wants to move Ferdinand Marcos’s body to a cemetery for heroes. It’s an affront to our history.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/07/opinion/a-heros-burial-for-a-long-dead-dictator.html', 'snippet': 'The Philippines’ new president wants to move Ferdinand Marcos’s body to a cemetery for heroes. It’s an affront to our history.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MANILA — Mornings in the Philippines reveal bodies dumped outside slums. Averaging 13 a day, nearly 2,000 in the last two months, the bodies are hung with cardboard labels: purse snatcher, drug pusher, addict. The authorities decline to investigate. These are the casualties of a controversial crusade against crime that was the subject of the recent spat between the country’s new president, Rodrigo Duterte, and Barack Obama, which devolved into crude insults (by Mr. Duterte) and canceled meetings (by Mr. Obama).', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/08/opinion/08syjuco/08syjuco-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 427, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/09/08/opinion/0