06T10:33:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Miguel Syjuco', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Miguel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Syjuco', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/2ee99045-e96a-55b2-a52c-bc3a746af50f', 'word_count': 895, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2ee99045-e96a-55b2-a52c-bc3a746af50f'}, {'abstract': 'Does his country really want a divisive arsonist fighting its fires?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/07/opinion/nicolas-sarkozy-is-back-but-france-has-changed.html', 'snippet': 'Does his country really want a divisive arsonist fighting its fires?', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — One of the most popular items on the social networks as Nicolas Sarkozy opened his new presidential bid last month was a video of an interview he gave to the French news channel BFM-TV on March 8, 2012. Still president then, Mr. Sarkozy was seeking a second five-year term, with François Hollande running against him. Asked whether he would quit politics if he lost, he firmly answered, “Yes.” The journalist was taken aback. “Will you quit politics?” he asked again, incredulous. “You can ask me a third time,” the president shot back. “I’m telling you: yes.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/07/opinion/07kauffmann-2/07kauffmann-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/09/07/opinion/07kauffmann-2/07kauffmann-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/07/opinion/07kauffmann-2/07kauffmann-2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'cr