mbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Historic Building That Presented an Intriguing Architectural Problem', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'WORK Architecture Co', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'TriBeCa (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARCHITECTURE', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-09-07T15:43:23+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'T Magazine', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'subsection_name': 'Design & Interiors', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/1b360ba4-ffea-56de-b2fd-eee8a40d5e21', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/1b360ba4-ffea-56de-b2fd-eee8a40d5e21'}, {'abstract': 'The architects at WORKac were tasked with designing a penthouse that couldn’t be seen from the street. They succeeded, which is a shame: It’s pretty cool.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/07/t-magazine/design/workac-architects-obsidian-penthouse.html', 'snippet': 'The architects at WORKac were tasked with designing a penthouse that couldn’t be seen from the street. They succeeded, which is a shame: It’s pretty cool.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the Jones brothers, Joshua and John Q., built 93 Reade Street in 1857, it was one of the first cast-iron buildings in downtown New York. The brothers decided to show off a little — after all, they came from the very same family whose uptown house had inspired the phrase “keeping up with the Joneses.” The facade was a veritable wedding cake, incorporating just about every molding, capital and dentilled cornice they could get out of the D.D. Badger ironworks catalog, and when it was all bolted together, the brothers capped it off with a big arched pediment.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '120', 'source': 'The New York Time