seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2016-09-08T21:55:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'NYTNow', 'section_name': 'Briefing', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Workman and Sandra Stevenson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Workman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Sandra', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stevenson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a53df23e-7e97-5c73-851d-13a354cdab01', 'word_count': 935, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a53df23e-7e97-5c73-851d-13a354cdab01'}, {'abstract': 'Kirsten Johnson’s riveting documentary flits from Bosnia to Darfur to ground zero and zigzags across time, prompting reflection on the nature of her art.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/09/movies/cameraperson-review-kirsten-johnson.html', 'snippet': 'Kirsten Johnson’s riveting documentary flits from Bosnia to Darfur to ground zero and zigzags across time, prompting reflection on the nature of her art.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Cameraperson,” Kirsten Johnson’s transfixing documentary, defies easy summary. It’s unlike anything you’ve seen before, although if you remember films like Kirby Dick and Amy Ziering Kofman’s “Derrida”  or Whitney Dow and Marco Williams’s “Two Towns of Jasper,” some of the images may be familiar. Ms. Johnson worked as a cinematographer on those documentaries and dozens of others, but the footage she shares here doesn’t come from the finished films. It includes outtakes, offhand moments, snatches of time when the camera just happened to be running. “These are the images that marked me,” she says in an introductory note, and she has woven them together into a unique and affecting memoir.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 