es.com/2016/09/09/arts/design/zao-wou-ki-an-abstract-fusion-master.html', 'snippet': '“Zao Wou-Ki: No Limits” at Asia Society is the first comprehensive show of the artist’s work, which radiate energy and hypnotic power, in the United States.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The painter Zao Wou-Ki (1920-2013) is by now one of the best-known Chinese painters of the 20th century. It helped that he lived in Paris from 1948 on and that his gestural abstractions, which drew on Eastern and Western sources, enjoyed great success in Europe and the United States. By 1952 he had had solo shows in Paris and New York and went on to be collected by many museums on both continents. In New York he exhibited in the late 1950s and ’60s at Samuel Kootz’s gallery, an Abstract Expressionist stalwart.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/09/arts/09ZAO/09ZAO-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 792, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/09/09/arts/09ZAO/09ZAO-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 792}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/09/arts/09ZAO/09ZAO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 776, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/09/arts/09ZAO/09ZAO-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1552, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/09/arts/09ZAO/09ZAO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/09/09/arts/09ZAO/09ZAO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/09/arts/09ZAO/09ZAO-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Zao Wou-Ki, an Abstract Fusion Master', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Abstract Fusion Master', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Asia Society', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zao Wou-ki', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Zao Wou-Ki: No Limits (Exhibit)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-09-08T22:00:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roberta Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roberta', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/830e95a4-fb0a-50a7-8857-245ed3ccdbe6', 'word_count': 1237, 'uri': 'nyt://article/830e95a4-fb0a-50a7-8857-245ed3ccdbe6'}, {'abstract': 'Jeff Feuerzeig’s documentary charts the invention of an underage H.I.V.-positive hustler, and how the famous and nonfamous were taken in until the hoax was exposed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/09/movies/author-the-jt-leroy-story-review.html', 'snippet': 'Jeff Feuerzeig’s documentary charts the invention of an underage H.I.V.-positive hustler, and how the famous and nonfamous were taken in until the hoax was exposed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s sometimes said that forgers of old-master paintings are accomplished artists in their own right. A successful fraud — even if it’s ultimately uncovered — requires discipline, hard work and talent, and it’s hard not to feel at least a grudging respect for charlatans who fool the public and especially the experts. We don’t call them con artists for nothing.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/09/arts/09AUTHOR/09AUTHOR-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 324, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/09/09/arts/09AUTHOR/09AUTHOR-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 324}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/09/arts/09AUTHOR/09AUTHOR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 553, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/09/arts/09AUTHOR/09AUTHOR-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1107, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/09/arts/09AUTHOR/09AUTHOR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/09/09/arts/09AUTHOR/09AUTHOR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/09/arts/09AUTHOR/09AUTHOR-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: ‘Author: The JT Leroy Story’ Renders a Literary Charlatan', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Hoax Whose Mastermind Still Revels in the Myth', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Albert, Laura', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Feuerzeig, Jeff', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Author: The JT Leroy Story (Movie)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-09-08T22:02:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/83972ed5-df8a-53d4-abef-fc2f137aab35', 'word_count': 888, 'uri': 'nyt://article/83972ed5-df8a-53d4-abef-fc2f137aab35'}, {'abstract': 'A headstrong singer comes of age on the eve of Tunisia’s Jasmine Revolution.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/09/movies/review-as-i-open-my-eyes.html', 'snippet': 'A headstrong singer comes of age on the eve of Tunisia’s Jasmine Revolution.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I see those deprived of work, of food, of life outside the neighborhood,” sings Farah (Baya Medhaffar), a fiery 18-year-old Tunisian singer who lights up Leyla Bouzid’s film “As I Open My Eyes.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/09/arts/09ASIOPEN/09ASIOPEN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 337, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/09/09/arts/09ASIOPEN/09ASIOPEN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 337}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/09/arts/09ASIOPEN/09ASIOPEN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 575, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/09/arts/09ASIOPEN/09ASIOPEN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1132, 'width': 2017, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/09/arts/09ASIOPEN/09ASIOPEN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/09/09/arts/09ASIOPEN/09ASIOPEN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/09/arts/09ASIOPEN/09ASIOPEN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: In ‘As I Open My Eyes,’ Feeling a Revolution Coming', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'She Can Feel a Revolution Coming', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'As I Open My Eyes (Movie)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bouzid, Leyla (1984- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Medhaffar, Baya (1995- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-09-08T22:03:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b2bdb8c9-6ff0-586f-81ac-9a4113450650', 'word_count': 390, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b2bdb8c9-6ff0-586f-81ac-9a4113450650'}, {'abstract': 'This eight-episode thriller coming to Cinemax sets a grim Vietnam-era tale in a soulful Memphis.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/09/arts/television/review-quarry-cinemax-max-allan-collins.html', 'snippet': 'This eight-episode thriller coming to Cinemax sets a grim Vietnam-era tale in a soulful Memphis.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With “Quarry,” an eight-episode hard-boiled thriller beginning Friday night, Cinemax boards the mystery train once again. Following “Banshee” (crime in small-town Pennsylvania) and “Outcast” (horror in rural West Virginia), “Quarry” is another exercise in lurid pulp storytelling and sweaty regional flavor. It’s not as much fun as either of those earlier shows — it’s too slow and solemn, and its obligatory touches of baroque weirdness lack conviction. But the carefully maintained 1970s setting, a few good performances and a soundtrack full of Memphis soul at least partly redeem it.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/09/arts/09QUARRY/09QUARRY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/09/09/arts/09QUARRY/09QUARRY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/09/arts/09QUARRY/09QUARRY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/09/arts/09QUARRY/09QUARRY-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/0