.nytimes.com/2016/09/08/arts/television/narcos-netflix-season-2-episode-9-recap.html', 'snippet': 'Here we are, in the second to last episode of Escobar’s story, almost literally stuck in the mud.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The trouble with adapting biographies or historical events into a fictional narrative is that real life doesn’t unfold in a neat three-act structure — it lurches and wheezes through bursts of activity and stretches where little of consequence happens and it doesn’t usually end how a dramatist would prefer to write it. Over two seasons of “Narcos,” the rise and fall of Pablo Escobar has been documented in a heady rush of information, so much that Murphy’s ubiquitous voice-over has been necessary to cram it all in there. And that’s been a primary strength of the show: The scope of Escobar’s empire and the multifront effort to dismantle it isn’t easy to encapsulate in any medium. If any story could be said to benefit from meat-and-potatoes artlessness, it’s Escobar’s.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/02/watching/02narcos9/02narcos9-articleLarge-v13.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/09/02/watching/02narcos9/02narcos9-articleLarge-v13.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/02/watching/02narcos9/02narcos9-jumbo-v13.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/02/watching/02narcos9/02narcos9-superJumbo-v13.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'ima