urce': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/11/arts/11LOVETTE1/11LOVETTE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 397, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/09/11/arts/11LOVETTE1/11LOVETTE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 397}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/11/arts/11LOVETTE1/11LOVETTE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 678, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/11/arts/11LOVETTE1/11LOVETTE1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1355, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/11/arts/11LOVETTE1/11LOVETTE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/09/11/arts/11LOVETTE1/11LOVETTE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/11/arts/11LOVETTE1/11LOVETTE1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Ballerina Steps Out — as a Choreographer', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'They’re Her Steps, From Her Life', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York City Ballet', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lovette, Lauren', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'School of American Ballet', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'For Clara (Ballet)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-09-09T21:25:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gia Kourlas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kourlas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b3442ca1-7291-5ec6-b4e1-f1ea206b38a5', 'word_count': 1135, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b3442ca1-7291-5ec6-b4e1-f1ea206b38a5'}, {'abstract': 'New York seems keenly attuned to the 15th anniversary of the terrorist attacks, yet the classical music world is scarcely taking notice.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/10/arts/music/9-11-memorial-concerts-mostly-low-key.html', 'snippet': 'New York seems keenly attuned to the 15th anniversary of the terrorist attacks, yet the classical music world is scarcely taking notice.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In classical music, birthday celebrations and other anniversary observances sometimes seem strained, even ludicrous, marketing ploys. It is hard to argue with recognitions of round numbers in, say, multiples of 50. But something like Carnegie Hall’s 125th anniversary, which came in May? Borderline. And how does a 15th anniversary stack up against a 10th?', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/10/arts/10MEMORIAL/10MEMORIAL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/09/10/arts/10MEMORIAL/10MEMORIAL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/10/arts/10MEMORIAL/10MEMORIAL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/10/arts/10MEMORIAL/10MEMORIAL-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/10/arts/10MEMORIAL/10MEMORIAL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/09/10/arts/10MEMORIAL/10MEMORIAL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/10/arts/10MEMORIAL/10MEMORIAL-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '9/11 Memorial Concerts, Mostly Low-Key', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In the Classical Music World, Sept. 11 Anniversary Will Pass With Little Notice', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'September 11 (2001)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Choir of Trinity Wall Street', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Military Academy Band', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bargemusic', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "St Paul's Chapel (Manhattan, NY)", 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-09-09T21:28:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By James R. Oestreich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Oestreich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e0294297-c4aa-5a70-817e-53e6c5ab431a', 'word_count': 644, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e0294297-c4aa-5a70-817e-53e6c5ab431a'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Marshall has always painted only black figures: “If I didn’t do it, how else were they going to be seen?”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/18/arts/design/kerry-james-marshall-met-breuer-mastry.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Marshall has always painted only black figures: “If I didn’t do it, how else were they going to be seen?”', 'lead_paragraph': 'CHICAGO — Kerry James Marshall, whose highly anticipated retrospective, “Mastry,” opens Oct. 25 at the Met Breuer, is steeped in classical training more thoroughly than almost any painter of his generation. He’s spent hundreds of hours in figure-drawing classes and anatomical studies, honing techniques developed over centuries by idols like Veronese and Rembrandt, to “get up alongside them on the wall,” as he says.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '78', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/18/arts/18Marshall1/0918Marshall1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 516, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/09/18/arts/18Marshall1/0918Marshall1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 516}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/18/arts/18Marshall1/0918Marshall1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 880, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/18/arts/18Marshall1/0918Marshall1-s