alue': 'Editorials', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-09-10T01:40:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/0c9cd850-727d-5cfc-bfc8-a0078b34a61b', 'word_count': 586, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0c9cd850-727d-5cfc-bfc8-a0078b34a61b'}, {'abstract': 'The state’s Senate majority leader wants to impose the strictest voting ban in the nation on people convicted of a violent felony.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/10/opinion/virginias-republicans-turn-back-the-clock.html', 'snippet': 'The state’s Senate majority leader wants to impose the strictest voting ban in the nation on people convicted of a violent felony.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the last couple of decades, states have begun to recognize the particular viciousness of laws that prevent people with felony convictions from voting. Virginia Republicans, on the other hand, appear eager to return to the 19th century, when most of these laws were drafted explicitly to keep African-Americans away from the ballot box.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/10/opinion/10sat2web/10sat2web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 603, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/09/10/opinion/10sat2web/10sat2web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 603}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/10/opinion/10sat2web/10sat2web-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1019, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/