hool of Economics', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shafik, Nemat', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-09-12T11:01:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'DealBook', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chad Bray', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bray', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1a6d80d3-75e9-5d7f-b9bf-a31ad222b90f', 'word_count': 285, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1a6d80d3-75e9-5d7f-b9bf-a31ad222b90f'}, {'abstract': 'From the archives of the International Herald Tribune: The Roman Catholic Church may start electing cardinals in September 1966.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2016/09/12/1966-church-may-elect-cardinals/', 'snippet': 'From the archives of the International Herald Tribune: The Roman Catholic Church may start electing cardinals in September 1966.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TURIN, Italy — The Roman Catholic College of Cardinals may be turned into an election body, with the top bishop of each nation serving a limited term according to an authoritative report here today. The Turin archdiocesan weekly ‘‘Ti Nostro Tempo’’ (Our Times) hinted that a plan was under study at the Vatican. Such a change would throw out the centuries-old system in which popes alone choose cardinals and appoint them for life. Under the new system, the heads of the Church’s 53 national and regional bishop’s conferences would become cardinals. — New York Herald Tribune, European Edition, Sept. 13, 1966', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '1966: Church May Elect Cardinals', 'kicker': 'IHT Retrospective', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cardinals (Roman Catholic Prelates)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Roman Catholic Church', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Turin (Italy)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Vatican City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-09-12T11:16:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By International Herald Tribune', 'person': [{'firstname': 'International', 'middlename': 'Herald', 'lastname': 'Tribune', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/07808ff6-cab7-5924-90cd-5f96a61a5932', 'word_count': 101, 'uri': 'nyt://article/07808ff6-cab7-5924-90cd-5f96a61a5932'}, {'abstract': 'China’s tone-deaf leaders appear to think that taking a hard line against Hong Kong’s youth political movement can contain it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/13/opinion/can-beijing-stop-hong-kongs-separatists.html', 'snippet': 'China’s tone-deaf leaders appear to think that taking a hard line against Hong Kong’s youth political movement can contain it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HONG KONG — Two years after China’s leadership slammed the door on political reform for Hong Kong, six young candidates running on separatist platforms won seats in the Sept. 4 election for the territory’s legislature. The rapid rise of a youthful political movement intent on gaining more independence for Hong Kong is a direct result of Beijing’s tightening grip on this former British colony.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/13/opinion/13Lian-web/13Lian-web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/09/13/opinion/13Lian-web/13Lian-web-ar