type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cfbc4b5c-1a9e-5ada-931d-ace4f6af31d1', 'word_count': 764, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cfbc4b5c-1a9e-5ada-931d-ace4f6af31d1'}, {'abstract': 'From the archives of the International Herald Tribune: NASA astronauts streak 850 miles into space in September 1966.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2016/09/14/1966-a-trip-deep-into-space/', 'snippet': 'From the archives of the International Herald Tribune: NASA astronauts streak 850 miles into space in September 1966.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CAPE KENNEDY — Astronauts Charles (Pete) Conrad and Richard F. Gordon today [Sept. 14] streaked 850 miles deep into space — nearly twice as high as man has ever travelled before. ‘‘I tell you, you can’t believe it,’’ Comdr. Conrad radioed to earth as the Gemini-11 spacecraft attained the record height over Australia. ‘‘Just out of my window you can see the top of the world, all the way around… we have the whole southern part of the world out of one window.’’ To reach the record altitude, Comdr. Conrad and Lt. Comdr. Gordon fired the 16,000-pound–thrust engine of an Agena target rocket linked to their spacecraft for 25 seconds. As they sped back into the lower orbit, the spacecraft set a new manned speed record of 17,931 miles an hour. — New York Herald Tribune, European Edition, Sept. 15, 1966', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/15/blogs/15oldiht-1966-nasa/15oldiht-1966-nasa-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 737, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/09/15/blogs/15oldiht-1966-nasa/15oldiht-1966-nasa-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 737}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/15/blogs/15oldiht-1966-nasa/15oldiht-1966-nasa-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 834, 'su