: None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interview', '_id': 'nyt://article/a5ea3ceb-4ffe-524e-a1b7-c09ff173a43d', 'word_count': 888, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a5ea3ceb-4ffe-524e-a1b7-c09ff173a43d'}, {'abstract': 'Ms. Ryan plays a rapacious woman who ages from 19 to 64 in the course of the drama “Love, Love, Love,” which begins performances on Sept. 22.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/18/theater/amy-ryan-says-glamorous-parts-are-not-interesting.html', 'snippet': 'Ms. Ryan plays a rapacious woman who ages from 19 to 64 in the course of the drama “Love, Love, Love,” which begins performances on Sept. 22.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Amy Ryan didn’t want to do another play. “Oh god no,” she said on a recent morning in a cafe near her Brooklyn Heights home. “Too expensive. Too much hard work. It costs me a fortune in babysitters.” But when she read Mike Bartlett’s “Love, Love, Love,” which begins performances at the Roundabout Theater Company’s Laura Pels Theater on Thursday, Sept. 22, she succumbed.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/18/arts/18SNAPSHOT/18SNAPSHOT-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 1834, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/09/18/arts/18SNAPSHOT/18SNAPSHOT-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1834}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/18/arts/18SNAPSHOT/18SNAPSHOT-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 336, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/18/arts/18SNAPSHOT/18SNAPSHOT-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 671, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/18/arts/18SNAPSHOT/18SNAPSHOT-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/09/18/arts/18SNAPSHOT/18SNAPSHOT-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/18/arts/18SNAPSHOT/18SNAPSHOT-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Amy Ryan Says ‘Glamorous Parts are Not Interesting’', 'kicker': 'Snapshot', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘The Glamorous Parts are Not Interesting’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACTORS AND ACTRESSES', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Roundabout Theater Co', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ryan, Amy (1968- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Love, Love, Love (Play)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-09-15T12:00:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexis Soloski', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexis', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Soloski', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/28834b9e-a2a0-520e-81c8-d87dec88136e', 'word_count': 671, 'uri': 'nyt://article/28834b9e-a2a0-520e-81c8-d87dec88136e'}, {'abstract': 'Why are white men poised to get rich doing the same thing African-Americans have been going to prison for?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/15/opinion/jay-z-the-war-on-drugs-is-an-epic-fail.html', 'snippet': 'Why are white men poised to get rich doing the same thing African-Americans have been going to prison for?', 'lead_paragraph': 'This short film, narrated by Jay Z (Shawn Carter) and featuring the artwork of Molly Crabapple, is part history lesson about the war on drugs and part vision statement. As Ms. Crabapple’s haunting images flash by, the film takes us from the Nixon administration and the Rockefeller drug laws — the draconian 1973 statutes enacted in New York that exploded the state’s prison population and ushered in a period of similar sentencing schemes for other states — through the extraordinary growth in our nation’s prison population to the emerging aboveground marijuana market of today. We learn how African-Americans can make up around 13 percent of the United States population — yet 31 percent of those arrested for drug law violations, even though they use and sell drugs at the same rate as whites.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Jay Z: ‘The War on Drugs Is an Epic Fail’', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drug Abuse and Traffic', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jay-Z', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Crabapple, Molly (1983- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Drug Policy Alliance', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisons and Prisoners', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Marijuana', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sentences (Criminal)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mandatory Sentencing', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cocaine and Crack Cocaine', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-09-15T12:00:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Asha Bandele', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Asha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bandele', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/a6cb5f76-2e34-58a9-8aa3-fb6533063540', 'word_count': 384, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a6cb5f76-2e34-58a9-8aa3-fb6533063540'}, {'abstract': 'The fashion giant debuted his first see-now/buy-now collection on a city street; Boss Women and Derek Lam took another road.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/16/fashion/ralph-lauren-new-york-fashion-week.html', 'snippet': 'The fashion giant debuted his first see-now/buy-now collection on a city street; Boss Women and Derek Lam took another road.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the penultimate day (and the final night) of New York Fashion Week, Ralph Lauren shut down a stretch of Madison Avenue and cast his lot decisively with the see-now/shop-now, fashion-in-season-and-in-the-stores crusaders, whose cause — immediate gratification to reflect digital reality — has upended the traditional women’s wear season.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ralph Lauren Stops Traffic', 'kicker': 'Fashion Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ralph Lauren Stops Traffic', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'New York Fashion Week', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lauren, Ralph', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wu, Jason', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lam, Derek', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-09-15T12:00:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vanessa Friedman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vanessa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Friedman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/47f2c4b5-6309-57bf-9d30-46ad114d3c5d', 'word_count': 917, 'uri': 'nyt://article/47f2c4b5-6309-57bf-9d30-46ad114d3c5d'}, {'abstract': 'Newly elected to the Senate, Ms. Hanson, the leader of the One Nation party, also called for a ban on the burqa.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/16/world/australia/pauline-hanson-muslims-senate.html', 'snippet': 'Newly elected to the Senate, Ms. Hanson, the leader of the One Nation party, also called for a ban on the burqa.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SYDNEY, Australia — Two decades ago, Pauline Hanson prompted outrage when she said that Australia was in danger of being swamped by Asians in her first speech in Parliament.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pauline Hanson, Australian Senator, Urges End to Muslim Immigration', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Far-Right Australian Senator Urges End to Muslim Immigration', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Australia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Innis, Michelle', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hanson, Pauline', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Turnbull, Malcolm (1954- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslim Veiling', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and Africa Migrant Crisis', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-09-15T12:07:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Australia', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michelle Innis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michelle', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Innis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fac525b9-7b43-59ad-911a-bb28972c9729', 'word_count': 791, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fac525b9-7b43-59ad-911a-bb28972c9729'}, {'abstract': 'So far, anyway. The police and coroner have no leads about a heart found in a plastic bag near a gas station last month in Norwalk, Ohio.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/16/us/has-murder-come-to-town-this-heart-tells-no-tales.html', 'snippet': 'So far, anyway. The police and coroner have no leads about a heart found in a plastic bag near a gas station last month in Norwalk, Ohio.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Homicides are hardly common in Norwalk, Ohio, a community of about 17,000 near Lake Erie known as the Maple City. For years there were none, officials said, and last year the Police Department investigated only one. But all agree they have seen nothing like the latest possible case, which has stumped the authorities for three weeks: the curious matter of the bodiless heart.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/14/us/15xp-heart/15xp-heart-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/09/14/us/15xp-heart/15xp-heart-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': '