//www.nytimes.com/2016/09/15/arts/music/mercury-prize-skepta.html', 'snippet': 'This British rapper’s album “Konnichiwa” beat out releases by David Bowie, Radiohead and the 1975.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Konnichiwa,” the fourth studio album by the commanding grime rapper Skepta, was awarded the 2016 Mercury Prize on Thursday in London, beating releases by Radiohead and David Bowie for the title of best British or Irish album of the year. The annual cross-genre award — dubbed “the music equivalent to the Booker Prize for literature and the Turner Prize for art” by its organizers — comes with a prize of 25,000 pounds, or about $33,000.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/16/arts/16SKEPTA/16SKEPTA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/09/16/arts/16SKEPTA/16SKEPTA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/16/arts/16SKEPTA/16SKEPTA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/16/arts/16SKEPTA/16SKEPTA-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1368, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/16/arts/16SKEPTA/16SKEPTA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/09/16/arts/16SKEPTA/16SKEPTA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/16/arts/16SKEPTA/16SKEPTA-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Grime Rapper Skepta Wins the 2016 Mercury Prize', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rapper Skepta Wins the Mercury Prize', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Skepta (1982- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Awards, Decorations and Honors', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-09-15T22:24:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joe Coscarelli', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Coscarelli', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c6f1247c-bb7c-5adc-9eac-db1cdedccefb', 'word_count': 409, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c6f1247c-bb7c-5adc-9eac-db1cdedccefb'}, {'abstract': 'Johnny Coca visits the underground marketplace, one of his favorite spaces in the city, to reflect on heritage and craft.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/15/t-magazine/fashion/johnny-coca-mulberry-london-silver-vaults.html', 'snippet': 'Johnny Coca visits the underground marketplace, one of his favorite spaces in the city, to reflect on heritage and craft.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On a recent afternoon on London’s Chancery Lane, Johnny Coca, the creative director of Mulberry, was browsing the underground marketplace of the London Silver Vaults — a warren of around 30 subterranean shops that has become one of his favorite places since he moved here six years ago. “I think it’s a very unexpected place,” he said, conspiratorially. “It’s kind of like a jewel display for me, because y