t', 'value': 'Identification Devices', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-09-16T20:44:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Wines', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wines', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bbef9bf8-2a63-5ce0-9584-cfc5667009f0', 'word_count': 1214, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bbef9bf8-2a63-5ce0-9584-cfc5667009f0'}, {'abstract': 'In an exclusive clip from the new Netflix documentary “The 100 Years Show,” the artist Carmen Herrera makes a case for staying quiet.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/16/t-magazine/art/carmen-herrera-documentary-alison-klayman.html', 'snippet': 'In an exclusive clip from the new Netflix documentary “The 100 Years Show,” the artist Carmen Herrera makes a case for staying quiet.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last year, T ran a very popular story rounding up a number of female artists who have continued to create — and in some cases, have only just begun to receive critical attention — late in life, well into their 70s, 80s and 90s. The oldest among them, the Cuban-American minimalist painter Carmen Herrera, turned 101 in May, and if anything, her star has only risen since our story was published. She had a show in May inaugurating the new New York outpost of Lisson Gallery; and today, the Whitney opens a solo show focusing on her work from 1948 to 1978. It’s auspicious timing for the release, on Netflix, of a new documentary about the artist by the award-winning filmmaker Alison Klayman (known most recently for “Ai Weiwei: Never Sorry”), an exclusive clip from which premieres above. In it, Herrera makes the case for artists not talking too much about their work: “You cannot talk about art; you have to art about art,” she says, smiling (and lightly pounding a desk for emphasis).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Watch a 101-Year-Old Painter Argue Against Talking About Art', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Herrera, Carmen', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Klayman, Alison', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-09-16T20:51:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'subsection_name': 'Art', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexandria Symonds', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexandria', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Symonds', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4ad76382-3817-5db8-8db1-bfc14fa3aa4c', 'word_count': 342, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4ad76382-3817-5db8-8db1-bfc14fa3aa4c'}, {'abstract': 'Honor Molloy explores rage, dissolution, sexual perversity and family history with a bleak and penetrating acuity in this play at Origin’s 1st Irish Festival.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/17/theater/review-crackskull-row-an-irish-gothic-with-masher-basher-and-rasher.html', 'snippet': 'Honor Molloy explores rage, dissolution, sexual perversity and family history with a bleak and penetrating acuity in this play at Origin’s 1st Irish Festival.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You might expect the 2016 edition of Origin’s 1st Irish Festival to kick off with an upbeat paean to Ireland. Instead we have Honor Molloy’s grotesque but richly satisfying drama “Crackskull Row,” at the Theater Workshop.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xla