 'Bridgeport (Conn)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-09-17T18:30:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/835cc782-7898-5e4d-a397-d9b40e3b1d14', 'word_count': 1035, 'uri': 'nyt://article/835cc782-7898-5e4d-a397-d9b40e3b1d14'}, {'abstract': 'We kill millions of “pest” animals each year, without the science to justify it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/18/opinion/sunday/americas-wildlife-body-count.html', 'snippet': 'We kill millions of “pest” animals each year, without the science to justify it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Until recently, I had never had any dealings with Wildlife Services, a century-old agency of the United States Department of Agriculture with a reputation for strong-arm tactics and secrecy. It is beloved by many farmers and ranchers and hated in equal measure by conservationists, for the same basic reason: It routinely kills predators and an astounding assortment of other animals — 3.2 million of them last year — because ranchers and farmers regard them as pests.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'America’s Wildlife Body Count', 'kicker': 'Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'America’s Wildlife Body Count', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Conniff, Richard', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wildlife Services', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Livestock', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wolves', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AGRICULTURE DEPARTMENT', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fish and Wildlife Service', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Frontiers in Ecology and the Environment (Journal)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-09-17T18:30:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Conniff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Conniff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/dd9cb624-10a1-5e9d-ba4a-ecf5fc5c81d3', 'word_count': 1176, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dd9cb624-10a1-5e9d-ba4a-ecf5fc5c81d3'}, {'abstract': 'Four decades after the Sex Pistols, there’s a new crowd in the mosh pit.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/18/opinion/sunday/because-punk-still-rocks.html', 'snippet': 'Four decades after the Sex Pistols, there’s a new crowd in the mosh pit.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BOSTON — Forty years after its noisy arrival, punk rock has hit middle age. CBGB, the seminal New York punk club on the Bowery, has been closed for a decade, but there is a CBGB-themed restaurant at the Newark airport. In my hometown, Boston, the 148-room Hotel Commonwealth, which occupies the former site of the storied punk venue the Rathskeller (always known as “The Rat”), offers a punk-themed luxury suite — for about $500 a night.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/18/opinion/sunday/18usmani/18usmani-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/09/18/opinion/sunday/18usmani/18usmani-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/18/opinion/sunday/18usmani/18usmani-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, '