Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Grimes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grimes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/c2024bdc-9686-565f-9c19-cca8f616920d', 'word_count': 664, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c2024bdc-9686-565f-9c19-cca8f616920d'}, {'abstract': 'This city, which residents often refer to as the Buckle of the Bible Belt, may be on the cusp of decriminalizing marijuana.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/19/us/following-its-country-music-nashville-may-loosen-up-on-marijuana.html', 'snippet': 'This city, which residents often refer to as the Buckle of the Bible Belt, may be on the cusp of decriminalizing marijuana.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NASHVILLE — Willie Nelson’s famous habit of smoking marijuana is not seen as a badge of outlaw courage here anymore, so much as the frivolous foible of an eccentric uncle. A popular FM station disgorging the Boomer rock hits of yesteryear calls itself Hippie Radio 94.5; one of its sponsors is a smoke shop that incessantly hawks glass pipes and detox kits. Even mainstream country acts mention smoking marijuana now and again among the litany of acceptable American pastimes.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/19/us/19nashville3/19nashville3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/09/19/us/19nashville3/19nashville3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/19/us/19nashville3/19nashville3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'ju