se-curing powers that hindered their personal lives. Criminals who were “psychotic” and therefore could not be understood, only feared. Girls with eating disorders that appeared in one episode of a sitcom and then were cured after a heart-to-heart. Soap operas that reveled in the split personality — not actually a real disorder — as a plot point.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/19/opinion/19gordon/19gordon-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 337, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/09/19/opinion/19gordon/19gordon-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 337}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/19/opinion/19gordon/19gordon-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/19/opinion/19gordon/19gordon-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1151, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/19/opinion/19gordon/19gordon-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/09/19/opinion/19gordon/19gordon-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/19/opinion/19gordon/19gordon-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The TV Cure', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Television for What Ails You', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gordon, Emily V', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mental Health and Disorders', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-09-19T07:21:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emily V. Gordon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': 'V.', 'lastname': 'Gordon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/809f709c-75f1-5de6-8b73-e5f1a8a0846d', 'word_count': 1045, 'uri': 'nyt://article/809f709c-75f1-5de6-8b73-e5f1a8a0846d'}, {'abstract': 'Competition, stress and fears about the job market have college students trying to forget by drowning themselves in vodka.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/19/opinion/drinking-to-blackout.html', 'snippet': 'Competition, stress and fears about the job market have college students trying to forget by drowning themselves in vodka.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I call this the holy spirit,” he said, putting a concoction of blue Gatorade, vodka and God-knows-what-else in front of me — a single Jolly Rancher rested at the bottom of the bottle. I declined, he shrugged and passed the drink around to the two other women I was with.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/19/opinion/19oncampus/19oncampus-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/09/19/opinion/19oncampus/19oncampus-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'cre