', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Finney Boylan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': 'Finney', 'lastname': 'Boylan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/7c3f1aa1-875c-5119-9cc8-cb9974ddbd9e', 'word_count': 905, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7c3f1aa1-875c-5119-9cc8-cb9974ddbd9e'}, {'abstract': 'The type of information that gets through varies significantly depending on which candidate we’re talking about.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/20/opinion/campaign-stops/what-we-are-hearing-about-clinton-and-trump.html', 'snippet': 'The type of information that gets through varies significantly depending on which candidate we’re talking about.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This election season, most Americans are paying attention to the presidential candidates on a daily basis. That’s good news, but what Americans report reading and hearing about Donald J. Trump and Hillary Clinton has to make us wonder about the value of the hyper-prolonged, exhausting modern-day campaign process.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/20/opinion/20newportSub/20newportSub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/09/20/opinion/20newportSub/20newportSub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/20/opinion/20newportSub/20newportSub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/20/opinion/20newportSub/20newportSub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height'