ery Old Castle, Full of Contemporary Art', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2016-09-20T14:56:36+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'T Magazine', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'subsection_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By HETTIE JUDAH', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hettie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'JUDAH', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/0c7ef2af-34a1-5f33-8cd8-645449eeb3bd', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/0c7ef2af-34a1-5f33-8cd8-645449eeb3bd'}, {'abstract': 'In the ’70s, Gerald Laing left the Bowery for a remote, derelict castle he restored with his wife. Today, it’s dotted with the work he made there.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/20/t-magazine/art/gerald-laing-artist-kinkell-castle.html', 'snippet': 'In the ’70s, Gerald Laing left the Bowery for a remote, derelict castle he restored with his wife. Today, it’s dotted with the work he made there.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Wearying of the social whirl and turmoil of New York life in the late 1960s, the artist Gerald Laing did the unthinkable: He abandoned his successful studio practice in the city for a derelict castle in northeast Scotland. “Staying still and doing the same thing over and over would have been safe,” says the artist’s son Farquhar Ogilvie-Laing, who was born in 1970, while renovation work on the castle was underway. “It was considered absolute professional suicide for my father to move to the north of Scotland from New York when he was doing well. The Highlands in the 1970s were very remote.”', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/20/t-magazine/travel/Kinkell-ca