': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/31d74fcd-a550-5a70-9095-7c267fa6c2db', 'word_count': 1050, 'uri': 'nyt://article/31d74fcd-a550-5a70-9095-7c267fa6c2db'}, {'abstract': 'This two-and-a-half-inch-wide bracelet, designed by Tiffany & Co., is either ice cool or fiery red, depending on the angle.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/21/t-magazine/fashion/tiffany-rubellite-cuff.html', 'snippet': 'This two-and-a-half-inch-wide bracelet, designed by Tiffany & Co., is either ice cool or fiery red, depending on the angle.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the modern annals of fine jewelry, the rubellite beguiles. Unrelated to the ruby despite its rosy tone and similar name, it is a rare form of tourmaline, a semiprecious crystalline stone, often a blackish-blue shade. Rubellite, however, glows scarlet purple, like a luminescent raspberry. The artisans at Tiffany & Co. have crafted this nearly two-and-a-half-inch-wide cuff with 480 of the baguette-cut gems, 60 carats in all. With alternating lines of diamonds in a bold platinum armature, the bracelet looks entirely different — ice cool or fiery red — depending on the angle.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '112', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/25/t-magazine/25tmag-10qual_thing_tiffany-t_CA0/25tmag-10qual_thing_tiffany-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 833, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/09/25/t-magazine/25tmag-10qual_thing_tiffany-t_CA0/25tmag-10qual_thing_tiffany-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 833}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/25/t-magazine/25tmag-10qual_thing_tiffany-t_CA0/25tmag-10qual_thing_tiffany-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 738, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'c