s have served as the greatest inspiration for Paulina Olowska — painting, living and believing in a forgotten past.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For the Polish artist Paulina Olowska, Warsaw in the 2000s was a city of ghosts. Walking through neighborhoods, she would notice skeletal and darkened neon signs for shops that no longer existed, the legacy of a centrally planned “neonization of Warsaw.” She would pass shuttered storefronts with elaborate papier-mâché dioramas, a decorative tradition left over from a time when shops had little to display in their windows. She saw an expired idealism in the library for the blind across the street from her apartment; in the Modernist buildings, constructed with a \xadutopian society in mind and yet unlivable; in the defunct socialist monuments.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '226', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/20/t-magazine/paulina-olowska-slide-34TR/paulina-olowska-slide-34TR-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 756, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/09/20/t-magazine/paulina-olowska-slide-34TR/paulina-olowska-slide-34TR-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 756}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/20/t-magazine/paulina-olowska-slide-34TR/paulina-olowska-slide-34TR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 813, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/20/t-magazine/paulina-olowska-slide-34TR/paulina-olowska-slide-34TR-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1626, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/20/t-m