, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Sandra', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stevenson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/57d9f720-ccc2-5930-b7cc-10a14f2470f8', 'word_count': 1037, 'uri': 'nyt://article/57d9f720-ccc2-5930-b7cc-10a14f2470f8'}, {'abstract': 'Guidelines issued last year by the Justice Department stressed the need to hold individuals accountable for corporate misdeeds.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/23/business/wells-fargo-tests-justice-departments-get-tough-approach.html', 'snippet': 'Guidelines issued last year by the Justice Department stressed the need to hold individuals accountable for corporate misdeeds.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In response to widespread public criticism that the government has been too lenient with executives responsible for corporate crime, Deputy Attorney General Sally Quillian Yates issued tough new guidelines last year for federal prosecutors.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wells Fargo Tests Justice Department’s Get-Tough Approach', 'kicker': 'Common Sense', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Wells Fargo Case Tests a Get-Tough Approach', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wells Fargo & Company', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Frauds and Swindling', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Justice Department', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stumpf, John G', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Warren, Elizabeth', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-09-22T21:52:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By James B. Stewart', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/41e51314-df3a-5d5e-9154-1c24b8de1fff', 'word_count': 1474, 'uri': 'nyt://article/41e51314-df3a-5d5e-9154-1c24b8de1fff'}, {'abstract': 'Almost as soon as Ahmad Khan Rahami was taken into custody, an online campaign for two Elizabeth, N.J., men was started as a show of thanks. A backlash developed just as quickly.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/25/nyregion/elizabeth-new-jersey-bombs-gofundme-campaign.html', 'snippet': 'Almost as soon as Ahmad Khan Rahami was taken into custody, an online campaign for two Elizabeth, N.J., men was started as a show of thanks. A backlash developed just as quickly.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Sunday evening, the day after a bomb exploded on West 23rd Street in Manhattan, Lee Parker visited his friend Ivan White at his apartment in Elizabeth, N.J., to borrow some clothes for a job interview the next morning and to watch some football. Mr. Parker had been struggling to find work for the past few years; he was laid off after Hurricane Sandy and had been sleeping on a series of sofas belonging to friends for some time. The interview the following day was for work at a trucking company. Mr. Parker had mentioned to Mr. White that in addition to a new outfit, he wished he had a new backpack.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/25/nyregion/25BIGCITY/25BIGCITY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/09/25/nyregion/25BIGCITY/25BIGCITY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thu