62479-f846-5101-8a73-035491a77ebf', 'word_count': 620, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0a062479-f846-5101-8a73-035491a77ebf'}, {'abstract': 'This documentary looks at how and why a South Korean actress, Choi Eun-hee, and a once successful film director, Shin Sang-ok, landed in North Korea.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/23/movies/the-lovers-and-the-despot-review.html', 'snippet': 'This documentary looks at how and why a South Korean actress, Choi Eun-hee, and a once successful film director, Shin Sang-ok, landed in North Korea.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Every so often a documentary has such a head-shakingly bizarre story that alone makes it worth seeing. If you follow the news, this one may sound familiar: In 1986, a popular South Korean actress, Choi Eun-hee, and her ex-husband, a once-successful film director, Shin Sang-ok, entered the United States Embassy in Vienna and said that years earlier they had been kidnapped by North Korea — where they had been held against their will — and were now seeking asylum. It was stranger than fiction and also true (mostly, maybe).', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/23/arts/23LOVERS/23LOVERS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 470, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/09/23/arts/23LOVERS/23LOVERS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 470}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/23/arts/23LOVERS/23LOVERS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 802, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/23/arts/23LOVERS/23LOVERS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1604, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'l