ww.nytimes.com/audio/2016/09/23/arts/music/23popcast-pod.html', 'snippet': 'Shawn Mendes broke out on Vine. The next breeding ground for pop stars might be Musical.ly, a social music app for teens.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Shawn Mendes broke out on Vine. The next breeding ground for pop stars might be Musical.ly, a social music app for teens.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Popcast: The Next Teen Star Machine', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2016-09-23T14:36:59+0000', 'document_type': 'audio', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://audio/c61c74fa-1c23-5509-b29d-52248ffc72c6', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://audio/c61c74fa-1c23-5509-b29d-52248ffc72c6'}, {'abstract': '“The Hidden Life of Trees,” No. 5 on the hardcover nonfiction list, describes the way trees protect their young, communicate and develop communities.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/02/books/review/the-story-behind-this-weeks-best-sellers.html', 'snippet': '“The Hidden Life of Trees,” No. 5 on the hardcover nonfiction list, describes the way trees protect their young, communicate and develop communities.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Timber: “I speak for the trees,” the Lorax proclaimed. But maybe trees can speak for themselves, if we’d just shut up and listen. That’s the premise of a book by the German forest ranger Peter Wohlleben, “The Hidden Life of Trees,” that debuts at No. 5 on the hardcover nonfiction list and explains — in strikingly anthropomorphic terms — how trees protect their young, communicate through underground networks and develop sophisticated social communities, among other things. “When trees are really thirsty, they begin to scream,” Wohlleben writes. “If you’re out in the forest, you won’t be able to hear them, because this all takes place at ultrasonic levels.” Published last year in Germany, the book has already been a big best seller there, prompting a New York Times profile of the author last winter. (My favorite quote came not from Wohlleben — who once clarified to a German newspaper that “I don’t hug trees and I don’t talk to them” — but from a German literary critic eager to dismiss the idea that forestry was a particularly German obsession: “Yes, there’s ‘Hansel and Gretel,’ and, sure, if your marriage fails, you go for a long hike in the woods. But I don’t think Germans love their forest more than Swedes or Norwegians or Finns.”) Now that Wohlleben’s book has been translated into English, by Jane Billinghurst, other media outlets are also taking notice. “The thing that surprised me most is how social trees are,” Wohlleben told The Guardian recently. “I stumbled over an old stump one day and saw that it was still living although it was 400 or 500 years old, without any green leaf. Every living being needs nutrition. The only explanation was that it was supported by the neighbor trees via the roots with a sugar solution. .\u2008.\u2008. Trees are very interested in keeping every member of this community alive.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/25/books/review/25list-image/25list-image-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 264, 'width': 256, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/25/books/review/25list-image/25list-image-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 264, 'width': 256, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/25/books/review/25list-image/25list-image-thumbStandard.jp