, the subject of this play by Robert K. Benson, was one of the first women to become a New York City police detective.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One thing you had to say for Mary Shanley: She knew how to stop a pickpocket dead in his tracks. Ms. Shanley, the subject of “Dead Shot Mary,” was one of the first women to become a New York City police detective. Her story lives on in Depression-era newspaper headlines like “Policewoman Seizes Two Men Without Aid” (1936) and “Woman Detective Draws Pistol in Fifth Avenue” (1938). And now in this one-hour one-act, by Robert K. Benson, at Shetler Studios.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/24/arts/24DEADSHOT/24DEADSHOT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 406, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/09/24/arts/24DEADSHOT/24DEADSHOT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 406}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/24/arts/24DEADSHOT/24DEADSHOT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 692, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/24/arts/24DEADSHOT/24DEADSHOT-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1384, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/24/arts/24DEADSHOT/24DEADSHOT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/09/24/arts/24DEADSHOT/24DEADSHOT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/24/arts/24DEADSHOT/24DEADSHOT-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: In ‘Dead Shot Mary,’ a Pioneering New York Cop Walks the Beat', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Pioneering Cop  Walks the Beat', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gates, Anita', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Dead Shot Mary (Play)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kaliski, Stephen (1984- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McPhee, Rachel', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Benson, Robert K', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-09-23T15:19:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anita Gates', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anita', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gates', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ed5a33ef-984d-5c0d-8892-496b8c83a330', 'word_count': 362, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ed5a33ef-984d-5c0d-8892-496b8c83a330'}, {'abstract': 'Slipping in with crowds during intermission, known as second-acting, was once a rite of passage for students and starving actors. But theaters no longer turn a blind eye to the practice.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/25/nyregion/a-lost-art-on-broadway-sneaking-in-for-act-2.html', 'snippet': 'Slipping in with crowds during intermission, known as second-acting, was once a rite of passage for students and starving actors. But theaters no longer turn a blind eye to the practice.', 'lead_paragraph': 'During a visit to the city a few years ago, Linda Nenno, a former New Yorker now l