blican Party', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-09-26T16:47:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matt Flegenheimer and Jonathan Martin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Flegenheimer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Martin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f10b188f-06b2-51a3-8110-ca4ee9e12c08', 'word_count': 1151, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f10b188f-06b2-51a3-8110-ca4ee9e12c08'}, {'abstract': 'How Bill Clinton’s affairs, and the Republican nominee’s, shaped the media’s obsession.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/26/magazine/gennifer-flowers-donald-trump-and-the-making-of-the-sex-scandal-culture.html', 'snippet': 'How Bill Clinton’s affairs, and the Republican nominee’s, shaped the media’s obsession.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Saturday, Donald Trump once again used his Twitter feed to kick up a news microcycle when he tweeted that if Mark Cuban, a prominent Hillary Clinton supporter and occasional Trump nemesis, was going to attend the debate, “perhaps I will put Gennifer Flowers right alongside of him!” Trump’s campaign was quick to clarify that he was just making a point, but by merely mentioning the idea, Trump managed to reopen a chapter of ancient Clinton history that has played a mercifully small role in an otherwise sordid election cycle.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/26/magazine/26mag-flower/26mag-flower-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/201