e': 'Beckett, Samuel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Feldman, Morton', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Neither (Dance)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-09-28T16:43:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Schaefer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schaefer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/23b0fc0a-06a4-5f6b-81a6-3e1908997c2a', 'word_count': 156, 'uri': 'nyt://article/23b0fc0a-06a4-5f6b-81a6-3e1908997c2a'}, {'abstract': '“Securing the Shadow” focuses on an obsession with death in 19th-century America.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/02/arts/design/o-death-posthumous-portraits-at-the-folk-art-museum.html', 'snippet': '“Securing the Shadow” focuses on an obsession with death in 19th-century America.', 'lead_paragraph': '“It seemed like a resurrection,” said Sidney George Fisher, a well-to-do Philadelphian in 1858, upon seeing a miniature portrait of his recently deceased sister-in-law painted from a daguerreotype, a posthumous icon made as such imagery began to flourish in a society preoccupied with mourning.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/02/arts/1002WEEKAHEAD1/1002WEEKAHEAD1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/10/02/arts/1002WEEKAHEAD1/1002WEEKAHEAD1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/02/arts/1002WEEKAHEAD1/1