ng “Starry Night” by Vincent Van Gogh through the Lillie P. Bliss bequest in September 1941.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2016/09/29/1941-van-gogh-painting-acquired/', 'snippet': 'From the archives of the International Herald Tribune: The Museum of Modern Art in New York acquires the painting “Starry Night” by Vincent Van Gogh through the Lillie P. Bliss bequest in September 1941.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Museum of Modern Art, 11 West Fifty-third Street, announced yesterday the acquisition of a painting by Vincent Van Gogh, called “Starry Night.” The work, an oil painting measuring about 29 by 35 inches, was acquired by the museum through the Lillie P. Bliss bequest. “Starry Night,” the announcement said, is the first Van Gogh to be acquired by a New York museum, and one of the most important paintings obtained by the Museum of Modern Art. It was painted in 1889 in St. Rémy in the artist’s late style, and consists of a composition of cypress trees in the foreground with a village and hills in a distance beneath a sky with explosive stars and swirling milky way. The picture is comparatively little known in New York. — New York Herald Tribune, Sept. 30, 1941', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/30/blogs/30oldiht-1941-vangogh/30oldiht-1941-vangogh-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/09/30/blogs/30oldiht-1941-vangogh/30oldiht-1941-vangogh-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/30/blogs/30oldiht-1941-vangogh/30oldiht-1941-vangogh-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None