tname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nuwer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4bf2bc7d-9afd-540d-86e0-881bb4b81139', 'word_count': 477, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4bf2bc7d-9afd-540d-86e0-881bb4b81139'}, {'abstract': 'This year’s lineup, in films like “13th,” “I, Daniel Blake” and “Toni Erdmann,” largely focuses on fights for recognition and self-determination.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/30/movies/at-the-new-york-film-festival-a-defense-of-human-dignity.html', 'snippet': 'This year’s lineup, in films like “13th,” “I, Daniel Blake” and “Toni Erdmann,” largely focuses on fights for recognition and self-determination.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The second most memorable scene in “Toni Erdmann” — which is to say one of the two or three most remarkable moments you will encounter on a movie screen this year — consists of an impromptu amateur performance of “The Greatest Love of All.” I hesitate to say anything about who sings that notorious anthem to self-reliance or under what circumstances, but I will note that one line stands out, capturing with an impossible blend of excruciating irony and disarming sincerity one of the movie’s great themes: “No matter what they take from me, they can’t take away my dignity.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/30/arts/30NYFFESTJP1/30NYFFESTJP1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/09/30/arts/30NYFFESTJP1/30NYFFESTJP1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/30/arts/30NYFFESTJP1/30NYFFESTJP1-thumbL