e lodgings, introduces its newest place to stay.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/09/30/t-magazine/design/walled-garden-wilderness-reserve-kim-wilkie.html', 'snippet': 'Wilderness Reserve, a 5,000-acre estate dotted with an idiosyncratic set of rentable lodgings, introduces its newest place to stay.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In an English village, just inland from the rugged Suffolk coast, something close to Arcadia is being created. Under the watch of the revered British landscape artist Kim Wilkie, three historic estates — Heveningham, Sibton Park and Cockfield Hall — are being slowly re-imagined as one luxurious rural retreat. “The idea is to take what have been rather run-down and fractured places, where ownership has been split up, and restore them to what was a very balanced and harmonious whole,” says Wilkie of Wilderness Reserve, a 5,000-acre expanse of pasture, lake and woodland that’s dotted with an idiosyncratic set of rentable lodgings. Now the already up-for-rent Moat Cottage, Cartshed and electricity-free Hex Cottage have been joined by the Walled Garden, a palatial eight-bedroom bolthole wrought from one of Suffolk’s few surviving walled kitchen gardens.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/30/t-magazine/design/30tmag-walledgarden02/30tmag-walledgarden02-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/09/30/t-magazine/design/30tmag-walledgarden02/30tmag-walledgarden02-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/09/30/t-magazine/design/30tmag-walledgarden02/30tmag-walledgarden02-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 